.TH CYAML_GET 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cyaml_get \- navigate to a DOM node with a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_get(cyaml root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Navigate from root through a dot-separated path.  Return the node at the
destination.

Path syntax:
  - A dot ('.') separates the components.
  - A component that starts with '#' and then has one or more decimal
    digits indexes into a CYAML_LIST by position.  For example, "#0"
    is the first element.
  - The macro reads every other component as a literal
    CYAML_DICTIONARY key.
  - A bare '#' with no digits after it has no valid meaning as an
    index.  When the current node is a CYAML_DICTIONARY, the macro
    uses it as a literal key, exactly as it uses any other component
    that is not "#N".  Such a path addresses a key with the literal
    name "#".  When the current node is a CYAML_LIST, there is no
    literal-key fallback for a list at all.  The navigation then fails
    and the macro returns NULL.  cjson rejects a bare '#' for that
    case in the same way.
  - Escape sequences: '\e.' gives a literal '.' in the key, and not a
    separator.  '\e\e' gives a literal '\e' in the key.  A '\e' before
    any other character stays unchanged.
  - An empty component is nothing between two dots, a leading dot, or
    a trailing dot.  It has no valid meaning as a literal empty-string
    key, and none as a "#N" index.  This holds at every position in
    the path, and the navigation fails.
  - A NULL or empty path identifies root itself.  The macro then
    gives root.
  - A NULL root identifies no node.  The macro then gives NULL.

The reference that the macro returns is NON-OWNING.  It becomes invalid
when the caller changes or destroys the node that holds it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
root  Root cyaml handle.  It is normally a CYAML_DICTIONARY or a
      CYAML_LIST.
path  Dot-separated path.  It is a string literal or a char *.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A non-owning cyaml handle at the target.  The macro returns NULL when
root is NULL, and when a component along the path is missing.  It also
returns NULL when the node type does not fit, and when the path holds an
empty component.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cyaml port = cyaml_get(doc, "server.ports.#0");
if (cyaml_type(port) == CYAML_INTEGER)
    printf("%lld\en", cyaml_int_val(port));

/* An empty path gives root itself */
cyaml same = cyaml_get(doc, "");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_set (3)
